A||||14||JohnnyK||Headlight question. Need help fast!||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||03-18-2001||03:31 PM||||I am trying to get my left headlight to work, as it doesn't want to go up and down. The right one goes fine. This all happened when I disconencted them for winter, then  hooked them up ago a month ago, and they didn't work. I want to start finding the problem by giving 12volts to the actual motor. There are 3 wires. A green and a grey, which are on a plug, and then the seperate blue one. What ones do I want to give the power to? Thanks.. Someone also hook me up with some advice as to what could be wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||03-18-2001||03:36 PM||||Does the motor spin but the light wont move? Or does nothing happen at all? If the motor spins, you can rebuild it...VERY easy to do.<BR>As a temp fix, simply turn the motor by hand to raise and lower the headlight.<BR>(of course if that dosnt work, then you need to rebuild the motor...the 3 "pegs" that are in the internal lobe are probobly dust. Replace them...Rodney Dickman sells subsitute pieces, or you can use glue sticks! Yes it works, Ive done it on all mine and theyve worked GREAT since. M7F||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||03-18-2001||04:38 PM||||No no... The gears are fine. It's just now it doesn't seem to get power after I disconnected them for the winter. I don't get it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||AusFiero||03-18-2001||05:53 PM||||Have you checked to see if power is getting to the area...Use a multimeter or plug another actuator motor into the left side to see if it works. <BR>Disconnecting for winter may have loosened or damaged the wires in the plug.<BR>Only a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||03-18-2001||06:13 PM||||If you crank the headlight up manually, will it retract on its own?  If so, suspect the relay or connections at the relay.<P>If the headlight won't go up or down, another possibility is worn or dirty motor brushes or commutator.  This seems to be less common than gear failure, but I've heard it happens.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||JohnnyK||03-18-2001||07:32 PM||||It seems power is getting there. I connected a multi meter to random wires up there (well, the blue green and grey) and got 12 volts.. it won't go down on it's own. i don't think it'll go up either. Doesn't even make a noise.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||03-18-2001||08:20 PM||||sounds like the brushes on communtator are corroded. Id never disconnect them for any period. the once in a while use would have kept them clean.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Indiana_resto_guy||03-18-2001||09:38 PM||||Try another relay.||||reg||1
Z||000008||862M4inCA||03-19-2001||03:03 AM||||hrm....sounds like maybe a dead spot in the motor somewhere. Have you tried hooking all the electrical connections up turning the lights on and helping the motor by giving the manual crank a turn? I know when I have mine connected (usually disconnected since they work intermittantly until I replace the brush assy.)they sometimes stick, and after giving the crank a turn they go ahead and spin up.<P>OR, you could always use a little "stone age" technology and give it a few convincing whacks with a hammer.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> j/k <p>[This message has been edited by 862M4inCA (edited 03-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||JohnnyK||03-19-2001||11:58 AM||||Yup.. tried helping it.. Nothing.. Remember, the passenger side one works fine.. There isn't a relay for each motor, is there?||||reg||1
Z||000010||862M4inCA||03-19-2001||01:40 PM||||On the 84-86 motors there are three relays used. One located on the left fender, and one attached to each motor. the one on the fender takes one signal from the headlight switch and branches it to the two motor relays. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||JohnnyK||03-19-2001||05:39 PM||||Where EXACTLY would the driver side motor relay be? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000012||862M4inCA||03-19-2001||06:10 PM||||The relay for the drivers side motor would be EXACTLY located to the right side of the motor (toward the radiator) attached to a "dogear" piece that is part of the complete headlight assembly. If you have problems finding the relay then it would probably also be helpful to know that the main relay (the one on the left fender) has wires going to each of the actuator (motor) relays, and from there the motor connects directly to those relays. What do you currently have the motor wiring plugged into?||||reg||1
Z||000013||JohnnyK||03-19-2001||08:04 PM||||what does it look like? There are 3 wires coming out of it. A blue one, which goes to a single plug (which I disconnected) and 1 grey and 1 green one, which plug into some box, where 2 other wires go into. Is that the relay you mean?||||reg||1
Z||000014||862M4inCA||03-19-2001||11:23 PM||||yes. that "black box" is a relay. Try changing that one with the one on the other motor and see if the problem moves to the other motor, if not then the problem most likely lies in the main relay located on the left fender, that is, if the problem is relay related.||||reg||1
